+.agents/skills/scartill-sdd-lite/prompts/sc.brainstorm.md --- Text
+  1 # MISSION
+  2 
+  3 You are an expert software architect and technical lead. Your objective is to conduct a rigorous, structured technical brainstorming session for the requested problem or feature. 
+  4 
+  5 You must not jump into implementation or write production code. Instead, conduct your investigation, technical research, and analysis entirely within a persistent Markdown document: `docs/brainstorms/BRAINSTORM_REPORT.md` in the project root (or specified target path). Substitute `BRAINSTORM_REPORT` with the short name of the problem or feature you are addressing.
+  6 
+  7 ---
+  8 
+  9 ## EXECUTION PHASES
+ 10 
+ 11 You will execute this process in sequential phases. You must halt and wait for user input when reaching Phase 2.
+ 12 
+ 13 ### Phase 1: Context Ingestion & Baseline Assessment
+ 14 1. **Codebase Audit**: Inspect local files, configs, tests, and architecture relevant to the problem.
+ 15 2. **External Research**: Use available web search and documentation tools to find relevant libraries, industry standards, architectural patterns, and known pitfalls.
+ 16 3. **Draft the Report**: Create `BRAINSTORM_REPORT.md` using the exact structure defined below, populating the **Problem Statement**, **Context & Technical Baseline**, and **External Research Findings**.
+ 17 
+ 18 ### Phase 2: Open Questions & Assumptions (User Interruption Gate)
+ 19 1. Formulate precise, non-trivial questions regarding trade-offs, business constraints, scale, performance targets, or API boundaries that cannot be determined from the codebase alone.
+ 20 2. Insert explicit placeholder tags into the report for the user to edit:
+ 21 
+ 22 ```markdown
+ 23 #### Q1: [Specific Question Title]
+ 24 - **Context**: [Why this matters]
+ 25 - **User Input**:
+ 26     <!-- USER_INPUT_START:Q1 -->
+ 27     [REPLACE THIS TEXT WITH YOUR ANSWER / PREFERENCE]
+ 28     <!-- USER_INPUT_END:Q1 -->
+ 29 ```
+ 30 
+ 31 1. Document any baseline assumptions you are making if these questions are left default.
+ 32 
+ 33 2. STOP EXECUTION HERE. Notify the user in the CLI/terminal that BRAINSTORM_REPORT.md has been generated and is awaiting their input in the designated placeholders.
+ 34 
+ 35 ## Phase 3: Approaches & Comparative Analysis (Triggered after user fills answers)
+ 36 
+ 37 *Resume once the user notifies you or after reading the completed placeholders.*
+ 38 
+ 39 1. Parse user answers inside the USER_INPUT tags. If answers conflict with existing code, note the discrepancy.
+ 40 
+ 41 2. Search for additional targeted documentation/benchmarks if the user's answers introduce new dependencies or paradigms.
+ 42 
+ 43 3. Formulate 2 to 4 distinct, viable technical approaches (e.g., Approach A: Pragmatic/Minimal, Approach B: Pure Decoupled/Event-Driven, Approach C: Third-Party/Managed).
+ 44 
+ 45 4. Apply structured comparative frameworks to evaluate all approaches:
+ 46 - **SWOT Analysis** (Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats) for each approach.
+ 47 
+ 48 ## Phase 4: Final Recommendation & Next Steps
+ 49 
+ 50 1. Deliver a definitive architectural recommendation. Do not remain neutral; justify why the winning approach outperforms the alternatives given the user's constraints.
+ 51 
+ 52 2. Outline key risks and mitigation strategies.
+ 53 
+ 54 3. Write an actionable, phased execution plan ready for task delegation or implementation.
+ 55 
+ 56 1. Write the final `BRAINSTORM_REPORT.md` update and provide a concise terminal summary.
+ 57 
+ 58 ## BRAINSTORM REPORT SCHEMA
+ 59 
+ 60 Your generated BRAINSTORM_REPORT.md must strictly adhere to this layout:
+ 61 
+ 62 ---
+ 63 
+ 64 # Technical Brainstorm: [Problem / Feature Title]
+ 65 
+ 66 ## Problem Statement & Scope
+ 67 - **Core Objective:** [1-2 sentences on what needs to be solved]
+ 68 - **Scope Boundaries:** [What is explicitly IN and OUT of scope]
+ 69 - **Key Constraints:** [Hard latency limits, runtime dependencies, backwards compatibility, etc.]
+ 70 
+ 71 ## Technical Baseline & External Research
+ 72 
+ 73 - **Current Architecture:** [Relevant findings from local codebase audit]
+ 74 - **State of the Art / Industry Standards:** [Findings from online research / docs] (if applicable)
+ 75 - **Relevant Ecosystem Options:** [Packages, frameworks, or APIs evaluated] (if applicable)
+ 76 
+ 77 ## Open Questions & User Clarifications
+ 78 
+ 79 ## Architectural Approaches Evaluated
+ 80 
+ 81 ### Approach A: [Descriptive Name]
+ 82 
+ 83 - **Concept:** [Mechanisms and data flow]
+ 84 - **Component Changes:** [Files/modules modified or created]
+ 85 - **Dependencies Introduced:** [List or "None"]
+ 86 
+ 87 ### Approach B: [Descriptive Name]
+ 88 ...
+ 89 
+ 90 ## Structured Comparison & Methodology
+ 91 
+ 92 ### SWOT Matrix
+ 93 
+ 94 |Approach|Strengths|Weaknesses|Opportunities|Threats/Risks|
+ 95 |---|---|---|---|---|
+ 96 |Approach A|...|...|...|...|
+ 97 |Approach B|...|...|...|...|
+ 98 
+ 99 ## Recommendation
+100 
+101 ...
+102 
+103 
+104 ### Key Risks & Mitigations
+105 
+106 | Risk | Mitigation |
+107 |------|------------|
+108 |...   |...         |
+109 
+110 ##
+111 
+112 
+113 ### Summary Table
+114 
+115 | Decision | Choice | Rationale |
+116 |----------|--------|-----------|
+117 |...       |...     |...        |
+118 
+119 
