+.agents/skills/scartill-sdd-lite/prompts/sc.code.review.md --- Text
+  1 # Code Review
+  2 
+  3 Code Review the current PR. Write the report to a new file here: `docs/codereviews/`.
+  4 
+  5 **DO NOT** use absolute paths in code review report. Use paths relative to the repository root.
+  6 
+  7 ## PR Review Mode
+  8 
+  9 Comprehensive GitHub PR review — fetches diff, reads full files, runs validation, posts review.
+ 10 
+ 11 ### Phase 1 — FETCH
+ 12 
+ 13 Parse input to determine PR:
+ 14 
+ 15 | Input | Action |
+ 16 |---|---|
+ 17 | Number (e.g. `42`) | Use as PR number |
+ 18 | URL (`github.com/.../pull/42`) | Extract PR number |
+ 19 | Branch name | Find PR via `gh pr list --head <branch>` |
+ 20 
+ 21 ```bash
+ 22 gh pr view <NUMBER> --json number,title,body,author,baseRefName,headRefName,changedFiles,additions,deletions
+ 23 gh pr diff <NUMBER>
+ 24 ```
+ 25 
+ 26 If PR not found, stop with error. Store PR metadata for later phases.
+ 27 
+ 28 ### Phase 2 — CONTEXT
+ 29 
+ 30 Build review context:
+ 31 
+ 32 1. **Project rules** — Read `AGENTS.md` and any contributing guidelines
+ 33 2. **Planning artifacts** — Check `docs/specs`.
+ 34 3. **PR intent** — Parse PR description for goals, linked issues, test plans
+ 35 4. **Changed files** — List all modified files and categorize by type (source, test, config, docs)
+ 36 
+ 37 ### Phase 3 — REVIEW
+ 38 
+ 39 Read each changed file **in full** (not just the diff hunks — you need surrounding context).
+ 40 
+ 41 For PR reviews, fetch the full file contents at the PR head revision:
+ 42 ```bash
+ 43 gh pr diff <NUMBER> --name-only | while IFS= read -r file; do
+ 44   gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/contents/$file?ref=<head-branch>" --jq '.content' | base64 -d
+ 45 done
+ 46 ```
+ 47 
+ 48 Apply the review checklist across 7 categories:
+ 49 
+ 50 | Category | What to Check |
+ 51 |---|---|
+ 52 | **Correctness** | Logic errors, off-by-ones, null handling, edge cases, race conditions |
+ 53 | **Type Safety** | Type mismatches, unsafe casts, `any` usage, missing generics |
+ 54 | **Pattern Compliance** | Matches project conventions (naming, file structure, error handling, imports) |
+ 55 | **Security** | Injection, auth gaps, secret exposure, SSRF, path traversal, XSS |
+ 56 | **Performance** | N+1 queries, missing indexes, unbounded loops, memory leaks, large payloads |
+ 57 | **Completeness** | Missing tests, missing error handling, incomplete migrations, missing docs |
+ 58 | **Maintainability** | Dead code, magic numbers, deep nesting, unclear naming, missing types |
+ 59 
+ 60 Assign severity to each finding:
+ 61 
+ 62 | Severity | Meaning | Action |
+ 63 |---|---|---|
+ 64 | **CRITICAL** | Security vulnerability or data loss risk | Must fix before merge |
+ 65 | **HIGH** | Bug or logic error likely to cause issues | Should fix before merge |
+ 66 | **MEDIUM** | Code quality issue or missing best practice | Fix recommended |
+ 67 | **LOW** | Style nit or minor suggestion | Optional |
+ 68 
+ 69 ### Phase 4 — VALIDATE
+ 70 
+ 71 Run available validation commands:
+ 72 
+ 73 Detect the project type from config files (`package.json`, `Cargo.toml`, `go.mod`, `pyproject.toml`, etc.), then run the appropriate commands:
+ 74 
+ 75 **Node.js / TypeScript** (has `package.json`):
+ 76 ```bash
+ 77 npm run typecheck 2>/dev/null || npx tsc --noEmit 2>/dev/null  # Type check
+ 78 npm run lint                                                    # Lint
+ 79 npm test                                                        # Tests
+ 80 npm run build                                                   # Build
+ 81 ```
+ 82 
+ 83 **Rust** (has `Cargo.toml`):
+ 84 ```bash
+ 85 cargo clippy -- -D warnings  # Lint
+ 86 cargo test                   # Tests
+ 87 cargo build                  # Build
+ 88 ```
+ 89 
+ 90 **Go** (has `go.mod`):
+ 91 ```bash
+ 92 go vet ./...    # Lint
+ 93 go test ./...   # Tests
+ 94 go build ./...  # Build
+ 95 ```
+ 96 
+ 97 **Python** (has `pyproject.toml` / `setup.py`):
+ 98 ```bash
+ 99 pytest  # Tests
+100 ```
+101 
+102 Run only the commands that apply to the detected project type. Record pass/fail for each.
+103 
+104 ### Phase 5 — DECIDE
+105 
+106 Form recommendation based on findings:
+107 
+108 | Condition | Decision |
+109 |---|---|
+110 | Zero CRITICAL/HIGH issues, validation passes | **APPROVE** |
+111 | Only MEDIUM/LOW issues, validation passes | **APPROVE** with comments |
+112 | Any HIGH issues or validation failures | **REQUEST CHANGES** |
+113 | Any CRITICAL issues | **BLOCK** — must fix before merge |
+114 
+115 Special cases:
+116 - Draft PR → Always use **COMMENT** (not approve/block)
+117 - Only docs/config changes → Lighter review, focus on correctness
+118 - Explicit `--approve` or `--request-changes` flag → Override decision (but still report all findings)
+119 
+120 ### Phase 6 — REPORT
+121 
+122 Create review artifact at `./docs/codereviews/pr-<NUMBER>-review.md` :
+123 
+124 ```markdown
+125 # PR Review: #<NUMBER> — <TITLE>
+126 
+127 **Reviewed**: <date>
+128 **Author**: <author>
+129 **Branch**: <head> → <base>
+130 **Decision**: APPROVE | REQUEST CHANGES | BLOCK
+131 
+132 ## Product & User Summary
+133 - **The "Why" & "What"**: <2–3 sentences describing the user problem solved or business purpose>
+134 - **Key User-Facing & Behavioral Changes**: <bullet points detailing UX, workflow, config, API, or behavioral changes>
+135 - **Risk Assessment & Migration Notes**: <breaking changes, feature flags, required env vars, DB migrations, or rollback considerations>
+136 - **Testing Hints for QA**:
+137   1. <Explicit scenario 1 stakeholders/QA should verify>
+138   2. <Explicit scenario 2 stakeholders/QA should verify>
+139 
+140 ## Technical Summary
+141 <1-2 sentence overall technical assessment>
+142 
+143 ## Findings
+144 
+145 ### CRITICAL
+146 <findings or "None">
+147 
+148 ### HIGH
+149 <findings or "None">
+150 
+151 ### MEDIUM
+152 <findings or "None">
+153 
+154 ### LOW
+155 <findings or "None">
+156 
+157 ## Validation Results
+158 
+159 | Check | Result |
+160 |---|---|
+161 | Type check | Pass / Fail / Skipped |
+162 | Lint | Pass / Fail / Skipped |
+163 | Tests | Pass / Fail / Skipped |
+164 | Build | Pass / Fail / Skipped |
+165 
+166 ## Files Reviewed
+167 <list of files with change type: Added/Modified/Deleted>
+168 ```
+169 
+170 ### Phase 7 — PUBLISH
+171 
+172 Post the product-focused summary and technical review payload to GitHub. The GitHub review comment MUST include the Product & QA section so Product Managers and QA receive clear, actionable verification guidance:
+173 
+174 ```bash
+175 # Prepare review payload body:
+176 # ### Product & User Summary
+177 # - **The "Why" & "What"**: ...
+178 # - **Key User-Facing & Behavioral Changes**: ...
+179 # - **Risk Assessment & Migration Notes**: ...
+180 # - **Testing Hints for QA**:
+181 #   1. <Scenario 1>
+182 #   2. <Scenario 2>
+183 # 
+184 # ### Technical Review & Decision: <APPROVE | REQUEST CHANGES | BLOCK>
+185 # <Summary of critical/high findings or confirmation that validation passed>
+186 
+187 # If APPROVE
+188 gh pr review <NUMBER> --approve --body "<product summary + technical review body>"
+189 
+190 # If REQUEST CHANGES
+191 gh pr review <NUMBER> --request-changes --body "<product summary + required fixes>"
+192 
+193 # If COMMENT only (draft PR or informational)
+194 gh pr review <NUMBER> --comment --body "<product summary + review notes>"
+195 ```
+196 
+197 For inline comments on specific lines, use the GitHub review comments API:
+198 ```bash
+199 gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<NUMBER>/comments" \
+200   -f body="<comment>" \
+201   -f path="<file>" \
+202   -F line=<line-number> \
+203   -f side="RIGHT" \
+204   -f commit_id="$(gh pr view <NUMBER> --json headRefOid --jq .headRefOid)"
+205 ```
+206 
+207 Alternatively, post a single review with multiple inline comments at once:
+208 ```bash
+209 gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<NUMBER>/reviews" \
+210   -f event="COMMENT" \
+211   -f body="<overall summary>" \
+212   --input comments.json  # [{"path": "file", "line": N, "body": "comment"}, ...]
+213 ```
+214 
+215 ### Phase 8 — OUTPUT
+216 
+217 Report to user:
+218 
+219 ```
+220 PR #<NUMBER>: <TITLE>
+221 Decision: <APPROVE|REQUEST_CHANGES|BLOCK>
+222 
+223 Issues: <critical_count> critical, <high_count> high, <medium_count> medium, <low_count> low
+224 Validation: <pass_count>/<total_count> checks passed
+225 
+226 Artifacts:
+227   Review: ./docs/codereviews/pr-<NUMBER>-review.md
+228   GitHub: <PR URL>
+229 
+230 Next steps:
+231   - <contextual suggestions based on decision>
+232 ```
+233 
+234 ---
+235 
+236 ## Edge Cases
+237 
+238 - **No `gh` CLI**: Fall back to local-only review (read the diff, skip GitHub publish). Warn user.
+239 - **Diverged branches**: Suggest `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/<base>` before review.
+240 - **Large PRs (>50 files)**: Warn about review scope. Focus on source changes first, then tests, then config/docs.
+241 
+
+.agents/skills/scartill-sdd-lite/prompts/sc.critique.spec.md --- Text
+  1 ## Goal
+  2 
+  3 Challenge the specification and implementation plan through two distinct expert lenses BEFORE committing to implementation. The **Product Lens** evaluates whether the right problem is being solved in the right way for users. The **Engineering Lens** evaluates whether the technical approach is sound, scalable, and free of hidden risks. This dual review prevents costly mid-implementation pivots and catches strategic and technical blind spots early.
+  4 
+  5 ## Operating Constraints
+  6 
+  7 **CONSTRUCTIVE CHALLENGE**: The goal is to strengthen the spec and plan, not to block progress. Every critique item must include a constructive suggestion for improvement.
+  8 
+  9 1. **Product Lens Review** (CEO/Product Lead Perspective):
+ 10 
+ 11    Adopt the mindset of an experienced product leader who cares deeply about user value, market fit, and business impact. Evaluate:
+ 12 
+ 13    #### 1a. Problem Validation
+ 14    - Is the problem statement clear and well-defined?
+ 15    - Is this solving a real user pain point, or is it a solution looking for a problem?
+ 16    - What evidence supports the need for this feature? (user research, data, customer requests)
+ 17    - Is the scope appropriate — not too broad (trying to do everything) or too narrow (missing the core value)?
+ 18 
+ 19    #### 1b. User Value Assessment
+ 20    - Does every user story deliver tangible user value?
+ 21    - Are the acceptance criteria written from the user's perspective (outcomes, not implementation)?
+ 22    - Is the user journey complete — or are there gaps where users would get stuck?
+ 23    - What's the simplest version that would deliver 80% of the value? (MVP analysis)
+ 24    - Are there unnecessary features that add complexity without proportional value?
+ 25 
+ 26    #### 1c. Alternative Approaches
+ 27    - Could a simpler solution achieve the same outcome?
+ 28    - Are there existing tools, libraries, or services that could replace custom implementation?
+ 29    - What would a competitor's approach look like?
+ 30    - What would happen if this feature were NOT built? What's the cost of inaction?
+ 31 
+ 32    #### 1d. Edge Cases & User Experience
+ 33    - What happens when things go wrong? (error states, empty states, loading states)
+ 34    - How does this feature interact with existing functionality?
+ 35    - Are accessibility considerations addressed?
+ 36    - Is the feature discoverable and intuitive?
+ 37    - What are the onboarding/migration implications for existing users?
+ 38 
+ 39    #### 1e. Success Measurement
+ 40    - Are the success criteria measurable and time-bound?
+ 41    - How will you know if this feature is successful after launch?
+ 42    - What metrics should be tracked?
+ 43    - What would trigger a rollback decision?
+ 44 
+ 45 2. **Engineering Lens Review** (Staff Engineer Perspective):
+ 46 
+ 47    Adopt the mindset of a senior staff engineer who has seen projects fail due to hidden technical risks. Evaluate:
+ 48 
+ 49    #### 2a. Architecture Soundness
+ 50    - Does the architecture follow established patterns for this type of system?
+ 51    - Are boundaries and interfaces well-defined (separation of concerns)?
+ 52    - Is the architecture testable at each layer?
+ 53    - Are there circular dependencies or tight coupling risks?
+ 54    - Does the architecture support future evolution without major refactoring?
+ 55 
+ 56    #### 2b. Failure Mode Analysis
+ 57    - What are the most likely failure modes? (network failures, data corruption, resource exhaustion)
+ 58    - How does the system degrade gracefully under each failure mode?
+ 59    - What happens under peak load? Is there a scaling bottleneck?
+ 60    - What are the blast radius implications — can a failure in this feature affect other parts of the system?
+ 61    - Are retry, timeout, and circuit-breaker strategies defined?
+ 62 
+ 63    #### 2c. Security & Privacy Review
+ 64    - What is the threat model? What attack vectors does this feature introduce?
+ 65    - Are trust boundaries clearly defined (user input, API responses, third-party data)?
+ 66    - Is sensitive data handled appropriately (encryption, access control, retention)?
+ 67    - Are there compliance implications (GDPR, SOC2, HIPAA)?
+ 68    - Is the principle of least privilege followed?
+ 69 
+ 70    #### 2d. Performance & Scalability
+ 71    - Are there potential bottlenecks in the data flow?
+ 72    - What are the expected data volumes? Will the design handle 10x growth?
+ 73    - Are caching strategies appropriate and cache invalidation well-defined?
+ 74    - Are database queries optimized (indexing, pagination, query complexity)?
+ 75    - Are there resource-intensive operations that should be async or batched?
+ 76 
+ 77    #### 2e. Testing Strategy
+ 78    - Is the testing plan comprehensive (unit, integration, E2E)?
+ 79    - Are the critical paths identified for priority testing?
+ 80    - Is the test data strategy realistic?
+ 81    - Are there testability concerns (hard-to-mock dependencies, race conditions)?
+ 82    - Is the test coverage target appropriate for the risk level?
+ 83 
+ 84    #### 2f. Operational Readiness
+ 85    - Is observability planned (logging, metrics, tracing)?
+ 86    - Are alerting thresholds defined?
+ 87    - Is there a rollback strategy?
+ 88    - Are database migrations reversible?
+ 89    - Is the deployment strategy clear (blue-green, canary, feature flags)?
+ 90 
+ 91    #### 2g. Dependencies & Integration Risks
+ 92    - Are third-party dependencies well-understood (stability, licensing, maintenance)?
+ 93    - Are integration points with existing systems well-defined?
+ 94    - What happens if an external service is unavailable?
+ 95    - Are API versioning and backward compatibility considered?
+ 96 
+ 97 3. **Cross-Lens Synthesis**:
+ 98    Identify items where both lenses converge (these are highest priority):
+ 99    - Product simplification that also reduces engineering risk
+100    - Engineering constraints that aff
+
+[Diff truncated to avoid CLI limits]
